---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageTitle from '../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Apparatus from '../components/Apparatus.astro';
import CohortStrip from '../components/figures/CohortStrip.astro';
import Corridor from '../components/figures/Corridor.astro';
import DirectionChart from '../components/figures/DirectionChart.astro';
import JanghanWitnesses from '../components/figures/JanghanWitnesses.astro';
import JanghanBylines from '../components/figures/JanghanBylines.astro';
import HallyuCoverage from '../components/figures/HallyuCoverage.astro';
---
<Base title="Visualizations" current="visualizations"
  description="Research figures from three Digital Humanities projects, and the shared grammar that makes them readable: how uncertainty, single-source evidence and documented absence are drawn rather than footnoted.">
  <PageTitle title="Visualizations"
    lede="Every figure here is an argument from a documented dataset, built to show what the sources can support, including their gaps, and not to decorate. They share one visual grammar, set out first, so that a reader meeting any of them in the middle of a project already knows how to read it." />

  <section class="band band--flush">
    <div class="wrap">

      <section class="section first" id="how-to-read">
        <h2><span class="mark">How to read these figures</span>A mark says how good the evidence is, not how important the thing is</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Colonial-era and premodern sources are fragmentary, contested and often hostile to the people they
            describe. Most computational work smooths that away. These projects draw it instead, using the
            same four marks everywhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grammar">
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A solid bar."><rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="var(--b2)"></rect></svg>
            <div><b>Solid</b><span>An explicitly dated record. The dates are attested in a source, not inferred.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A dashed outlined bar."><rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="none" stroke="var(--b2)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></rect></svg>
            <div><b>Dashed</b><span>A window derived from a floruit, an approximate date or a reign. It shows a range of possibility, not a life.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A hatched bar.">
              <defs><pattern id="hatchlegend" width="6" height="6" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)"><rect width="3" height="6" fill="var(--b5)"></rect></pattern></defs>
              <rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="url(#hatchlegend)"></rect>
            </svg>
            <div><b>Hatched</b><span>The record rests on a single source. It is in the dataset, and it is not corroborated.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A dotted horizontal line.">
              <line x1="2" y1="11" x2="118" y2="11" stroke="var(--void)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="2 5"></line>
            </svg>
            <div><b>Dotted, annotated</b><span>An absence shown in place. Where the evidence bar admits nothing, the figure says so on the axis rather than closing the gap.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Three rules follow from those marks. <b>Precision is recorded, never invented:</b> a date a source
            does not give is not supplied, so records read <span class="mono">704–787</span>,
            <span class="mono">fl. 671–695 ?</span>, <span class="mono">b. 1933</span> or
            <span class="mono">undated</span>, and an undated record is listed in a register but never placed
            on an axis. <b>A zero under stated criteria is a finding; an unsearched gap is not,</b> and the two
            are labelled differently wherever they appear. <b>Candidates examined and rejected stay in the
            dataset</b> as documented negatives, so that the boundary of a claim can be inspected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <Apparatus label="Why the grammar carries no colour">
          <p>
            Pattern, not hue, carries the evidential meaning, so every figure stays legible in greyscale, in
            print, and to readers with colour-vision deficiency. Colour separates only categories that have no
            evidential ordering, such as the six cohorts in Buddhist Bridges, and that palette was
            machine-checked for colour-vision safety before use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The gap this addresses is documented: a 2024 survey of 186 web-based Digital Humanities projects
            found that 122 use maps, 12 critically adapt them, and none visualises uncertainty. See Tommaso
            Battisti, <i>Classification of web-based Digital Humanities projects leveraging information
            visualisation techniques</i> (Zenodo, 2024),
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14192758">doi:10.5281/zenodo.14192758</a>.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Buddhist Bridges</span>India–Korea Buddhist exchange, 300–2026</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            From the <a href="/projects/buddhist-bridges/">Buddhist Bridges</a> prosopography: 43 documented
            mediators in a frozen, source-graded dataset. The cohort strip is the argument in one image, the
            route ribbons show the eight journeys whose intermediate stations are actually recorded, and the
            direction chart shows an inversion that happens only once in sixteen centuries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <CohortStrip />
        <Corridor />
        <DirectionChart />
        <p class="prose small muted">
          Each person opens to a citable record in the
          <a href="/projects/buddhist-bridges/explore/">explore view</a>, where every claim carries its record
          id and source key.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">長恨 (1927)</span>A magazine whose contents are contested</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            From the <a href="/projects/janghan/">長恨 edition</a>: four witnesses that divide the same
            magazine differently, and a corpus in which most texts carry kisaeng attributions while most of
            those identities remain unestablished. Both figures exist to keep a disagreement open rather than
            resolve it silently.
          </p>
        </div>
        <JanghanWitnesses />
        <JanghanBylines />
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Hallyu in the Indian press</span>What the archives can support</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            From <a href="/projects/hallyu-indian-press/">Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press</a>: the
            figure that has to come before any curve, because each database begins its full text in a
            different year and starts indexing online editions in 2017.
          </p>
        </div>
        <HallyuCoverage />
      </section>

      <p class="muted small prose closing">
        Charts are hand-built SVG on this shared grammar. There is no charting framework and no decoration:
        frameworks are what most often break a digital project a few years after it is finished. Every figure
        carries its finding as its title, a caption explaining its encoding and its reach, and its underlying
        data as a table.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .first { margin-top: 0; }
  .grammar { max-width: var(--w-measure); margin: 24px 0 28px; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .g-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 130px 1fr; gap: 24px; align-items: center; padding: 16px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .g-row svg { width: 120px; height: 22px; display: block; }
  .g-row b { display: block; font: 600 14px/1.4 var(--f-ui); color: var(--heading); margin-bottom: 3px; }
  .g-row span { font: 400 14px/1.6 var(--f-ui); color: var(--text); }
  .closing { margin-top: var(--s-6); }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .g-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 10px; } }
</style>
